WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.435
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.204
and Golden Globe
award-winning actress

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:06.105
for her work on the hit series
"Black-ish."

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:09.876
The eighth and final season
airs Tuesday nights, on ABC.

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:10.977
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.945
-How was your first day
at the clinic?

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:14.847
What?!
-So fulfilling.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:16.582
-We met so many different
kinds of people

00:00:16.582 --> 00:00:18.584
with such incredible stories.

00:00:18.584 --> 00:00:20.987
-See?
Oh, gosh, you guys get it.

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:23.389
-Thank you for the opportunity.
-No, thank you.

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:26.358
Are you kidding me?
So, same time next week?

00:00:26.358 --> 00:00:28.695
-Oh. You want us
to do this again next week?

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:32.098
-Uh, yeah.
Not in a, like,

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.499
"I'm making you do it"
kind of way.

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:35.568
This is, like, a fun thing
for us to do together.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:38.037
-Oh!
So, this was optional?

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:39.706
-Yeah.
-See, Vanessa? I told you.

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:40.974
-[ Chuckles nervously ]

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:42.808
-She thought we had to come
since you're, like,

00:00:42.808 --> 00:00:44.277
our boss and all.

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:49.616
-Oh, so -- so you guys are only
here because I'm your boss?

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:51.450
-Oh, I'm so glad you get it.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.586
A lot of people
would not be cool with that.

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:55.187
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:55.187 --> 00:00:57.489
our good friend
Tracee Ellis Ross.

00:00:57.489 --> 00:00:59.826
How are you, Tracee?
-Hi, Seth!

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:01.594
I want to make an entrance
like it's real,

00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:02.828
but you're not
gonna see my face.

00:01:02.828 --> 00:01:04.664
But the dress is so good.
Watch. Here we go.

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.700
-It's beautiful.
Oh, thank you!

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.802
It is a beautiful dress.

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:12.105
It is a beautiful color.
Let me tell you something.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:16.142
Nobody wears bolder
and more beautiful colors

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:17.877
than Tracee Ellis Ross.
Consistently.

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:20.547
-I love a color, I love a color,
I love a color.

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:21.680
Hi, Seth.
How you doing?

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.816
We're back at home.
What is this?

00:01:23.816 --> 00:01:26.886
-I know. I'm sorry
to flip the script on you.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:29.222
I promise, next time,
it'll be in person.

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:30.890
And, you know,
I'm a little worried.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.193
I want to get your assurances
that even though "Black-ish"

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:35.962
is coming to a close,
you will continue to be

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:39.199
one of our favorite guests on
the show in the years to come.

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:42.101
-You are assured.
And you don't have to apologize.

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:44.971
Seth, I also shut myself down.

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.173
Not because I got delta --
got delta --

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.142
got omicron --
I can't even keep it together.

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:51.477
Not because I got it,
but because I got scared again,

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:52.945
and everything's
just back at home.

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:55.081
It just is what it is.
-Yeah.

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:56.649
I mean, for the easily scared,

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:00.586
this has been a real
return behind the doors.

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:03.189
-Let me tell you something,
I'm back at home by myself.

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:05.324
[ Both laugh ]

00:02:05.324 --> 00:02:07.159
-Well, congratulations.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:09.562
Congratulations on eight seasons
of the show.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:13.132
Congratulations on a premiere
that had, let's be honest,

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:16.536
one of the toughest gets
for a sitcom guest star

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:17.670
you could imagine.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.271
You guys got Michelle Obama.

00:02:19.271 --> 00:02:21.073
-Yeah, called up my friend,
texted her, and said,

00:02:21.073 --> 00:02:22.241
"Hey, you want to come on down,

00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:23.976
do a little guest spot
on the show?

00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:25.144
You know,
just you got a little time,

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:27.079
just come on over and visit."

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:28.381
Yeah, it was really exciting.

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:30.316
What a way to start
a final season.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:33.319
She is the --
And she's such a great actress.

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.321
-Yeah.
-She was so good at improv

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.789
and just kind of
hanging with us.

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:40.993
So that was a really fun way to
start our final farewell season.

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:43.129
-So, you will confirm
that you did not make her

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:44.430
audition for this part.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.098
This was an offer-only
situation.

00:02:46.098 --> 00:02:48.367
-Oh, no, she auditioned, yeah.

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:52.639
Actually, it was funny --
I had Barack audition for her.

00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:55.574
And then, once he made it
through the first round,

00:02:55.574 --> 00:02:57.609
I was like,
"All right, we'll see the wife."

00:02:57.609 --> 00:02:59.144
[ Laughs ]
-Interesting.

00:02:59.144 --> 00:03:03.383
And so now, in the end,
Barack didn't get the part?

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.952
-No. As a matter of fact,
it's funny you say that.

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:10.255
He did not.
And -- But it turned out well.

00:03:10.255 --> 00:03:13.125
It turned out really well.
No, they're amazing,

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:14.660
and, no, they did not audition.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.596
As a matter of fact,
we auditioned for them.

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:20.066
We had to make sure they knew
we were up to par.

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:22.602
-Now, your producers
were aware of the fact

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:25.204
that you were friends
with Michelle.

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:28.408
Did they wait a full eight years
before asking you,

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:30.109
or has this been something
that you've had

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:33.579
to put them off
over each season?

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:36.382
-They did not wait eight years,

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.419
but this was the first time
I was willing to ask.

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:41.321
-All right, perfect.
Well, it did work out perfectly.

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.589
Your sense of timing
was impeccable.

00:03:43.589 --> 00:03:46.025
-Thank you.
It's not normally the case,

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:48.627
but I'll take it on this one.
[ Laughs ]

00:03:48.627 --> 00:03:50.830
-So, what --
When you look back at a show

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.865
that's been on this long,
what are the things

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:54.667
that you feel like
have changed the most

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.503
or the things that
you've enjoyed watching mature

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:59.471
over the last eight years?

00:03:59.471 --> 00:04:03.376
-The truth is, the most
important part of the show,

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:06.679
for me, and the joy and pride
really all comes from watching

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.415
Anthony's facial hair grow.
-Yeah, good.

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:11.617
-Some would think that it is,
you know,

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:14.853
the extraordinary experience
of witnessing and being --

00:04:14.853 --> 00:04:17.824
participating in the growth
of the children on the show.

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:20.793
Or maybe even -- Look at this.
-That's amazing.

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:23.329
-This is how I know
time has passed.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:26.165
The robustness
of Anthony Anderson's beard

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:31.670
showed us that, truly, anyone
cannot achieve what he achieved.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:35.073
-I think it showed
we all have room to grow.

00:04:35.073 --> 00:04:36.476
We all have room to grow.

00:04:36.476 --> 00:04:39.145
-That is exactly
what it tells us.

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:40.913
But I'm also left
with a lot of joy and pride,

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:42.915
'cause, you know,
we did some good work, Seth.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.117
-You did.
-It feels like we did

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:47.253
some good stuff, yeah.
-You mentioned the kids.

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:50.789
And you know,
I have many writers on my show

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:52.224
who are younger than me --

00:04:52.224 --> 00:04:53.558
I would say,
almost all of them --

00:04:53.558 --> 00:04:54.593
but they're not children.

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:56.195
But you actually worked
with kids.

00:04:56.195 --> 00:05:01.400
Like, cool, plugged into
pop culture, the young people.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.670
Was that helpful, in that it
made you feel young,

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:07.706
or depressing
because it made you feel old?

00:05:07.706 --> 00:05:09.241
-It was devastating, Seth.

00:05:09.241 --> 00:05:12.578
It's something that I'm still
in therapy because of.

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:17.483
I entered the show and walked
around mostly, on most days,

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:20.119
considering myself quite cool.
-Yeah.

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:22.522
-You know, youngish,
so to speak.

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:24.823
-Mm-hmm.
-But, no, the children

00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:27.727
on the show, I think they
actually played a lot of games

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:31.264
and would turn to me
to kind of ask me questions

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:33.066
just so that they could
laugh hysterically

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:35.001
about the things I did not know.

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:37.036
And then, I would explain
to them that, like,

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:39.639
it's their job
to keep me up to speed.

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:44.209
So my failure as a cool person
is on them.

00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:45.377
They have to be accountable
for that,

00:05:45.377 --> 00:05:47.847
which they also thought
was funny.

00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:50.649
But you know what gets sad,
Seth, is when you're actually

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:53.219
trying to convince someone
how cool you are.

00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:55.454
-Yeah.
-It's depressing.

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:58.023
-Well, let me tell you,
you bring me on that set,

00:05:58.023 --> 00:06:00.926
'cause we hung out 20 years ago
in New York City,

00:06:00.926 --> 00:06:03.596
you and I, Tracee.
We ran this town!

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:06.732
We ran this city.
-You have no idea!

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:09.234
You have no idea the places
I've been, the people I've seen,

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:11.037
and the things I know!

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:15.174
-Now, a lot of those nightclubs
are closed now.

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:18.377
They're cycling clubs now.
-Yes, yes.

00:06:18.377 --> 00:06:22.314
-Spin classes happen there.
I have more questions for you.

00:06:22.314 --> 00:06:24.917
And in the time --
in this commercial break,

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:27.186
I would just invite you,
as I will do,

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:28.554
to reflect on our youth.

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:31.224
We'll be right back with more
from Tracee Ellis Ross.

